body:
### Tested versions

Tested on Godot 4.4.1.stable

### System information

Windows 10, Godot 4.4.1.stable

### Issue description

Godot currently only supports saving PNGs in certain formats. If the image is already in one of these formats, all is well. However, if it is in an unsupported format it will be converted before saving, and this can result in losing alpha data. Unsupported formats are converted to either RGB8 or RGBA8 depending on whether alpha is detected with `detect_alpha()`:
```cpp
// excerpted from image_to_png() in drivers/png/png_driver_common.cpp
switch (source_image->get_format()) {
	case Image::FORMAT_L8:
		png_img.format = PNG_FORMAT_GRAY;
		break;
	case Image::FORMAT_LA8:
		png_img.format = PNG_FORMAT_GA;
		break;
	case Image::FORMAT_RGB8:
		png_img.format = PNG_FORMAT_RGB;
		break;
	case Image::FORMAT_RGBA8:
		png_img.format = PNG_FORMAT_RGBA;
		break;
	default:
		if (source_image->detect_alpha() != Image::ALPHA_NONE) {
			source_image->convert(Image::FORMAT_RGBA8);
			png_img.format = PNG_FORMAT_RGBA;
		} else {
			source_image->convert(Image::FORMAT_RGB8);
			png_img.format = PNG_FORMAT_RGB;
		}
}
```
This would be fine if `detect_alpha()` worked for all formats, but it does not. `detect_alpha()` only even attempts to detect whether there is alpha in the image for LA8 and RGBA8 formats. It automatically assumes DXT3/DXT5 have alpha. Images in all other formats, even ones that support alpha, are assumed to have no alpha.
```cpp
// excerpted from Image::detect_alpha() in core/io/image.cpp
switch (format) {
	case FORMAT_LA8: {
		for (int i = 0; i < (len >> 1); i++) {
			DETECT_ALPHA(data_ptr[(i << 1) + 1]);
		}

	} break;
	case FORMAT_RGBA8: {
		for (int i = 0; i < (len >> 2); i++) {
			DETECT_ALPHA(data_ptr[(i << 2) + 3])
		}

	} break;
	case FORMAT_DXT3:
	case FORMAT_DXT5: {
		detected = true;
	} break;
	default: {
	}
}

if (detected) {
	return ALPHA_BLEND;
} else if (bit) {
	return ALPHA_BIT;
} else {
	return ALPHA_NONE;
}
```

This causes issues when using an unsupported format like RGBAF, because it unnecessarily strips out the alpha channel. As a workaround, manually converting the image into RGBA8 before creating the PNG will keep the alpha.

### Steps to reproduce

Convert an image with alpha to either RGBA8 or RGBAF. Save it to some bytes with `save_png_to_buffer()`. Get it back as an image with `load_png_from_buffer()`. Notice that the image that started as RGBAF has lost its alpha channel, while the one that started as RGBA8 has retained it.

